    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>For most healthy adults, calcium supplements do little to prevent fractures.</strong> A 2026 BMJ review of 69 trials in 153,902 adults found calcium alone gave a 9% fracture reduction that was not statistically significant. Clear benefit appears mainly in frail, deficient older people, and in anyone who cannot reach about 1,000mg of calcium from food.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The 2026 result:</strong> calcium alone, any fracture, risk ratio 0.91 across 11 trials and 9,067 people, with a confidence interval running from 0.81 to 1.01 — it crosses no effect.</li>
        <li><strong>Why older reviews looked better:</strong> in 2015 the four trials at lowest risk of bias, 44,505 people between them, showed no fracture benefit at any site.</li>
        <li><strong>Where it does work:</strong> 1,200mg of calcium with 800 IU of vitamin D3 cut hip fractures in institutionalised French women averaging 84 years of age.</li>
        <li><strong>The India difference:</strong> average dietary calcium here is roughly 300 to 460mg a day against a 1,000mg target, so the deficiency case is met far more often than in the trial populations.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

