      <h2 id="fractures">Do calcium supplements actually prevent fractures?</h2>

      <p>
        In generally healthy adults, barely. That is the conclusion of the largest synthesis yet
        published: a systematic review and meta-analysis in <em>The BMJ</em> in 2026 that pooled 69
        randomised trials and 153,902 adults who were not taking osteoporosis medication.
      </p>

      <p>
        For any fracture, calcium on its own returned a risk ratio of 0.91 from 11 trials and 9,067
        participants, graded moderate certainty. A risk ratio of 0.91 sounds like a 9% reduction,
        and it is — but the confidence interval ran from 0.81 to 1.01, which includes no effect at
        all. Vitamin D alone came in at 1.00 across 36 trials and 92,045 people, this time at high
        certainty. Calcium and vitamin D taken together did reach statistical significance, 0.91
        with an interval of 0.84 to 0.99 across 15 trials and 51,126 participants, but the absolute
        difference was about 1.0% — below the threshold the authors had set in advance for clinical
        importance. Their conclusion was that the findings do not support routine supplementation
        to prevent fractures or falls.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is how that sits against the two reviews most bone supplement pages still quote.
      </p>

