      <h2 id="older-trials">Why did the older studies look more positive?</h2>

      <p>
        Because the weaker trials pulled the average down, and because dietary calcium and
        supplemental calcium were often discussed as though they were the same thing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The high-water mark was Tang and colleagues in <em>The Lancet</em> in 2007: 29 trials,
        63,897 people over 50, with fracture measured in 17 trials covering 52,625 participants. That
        analysis found a 12% reduction in fractures of all types, risk ratio 0.88 with an interval of
        0.83 to 0.95, and reported better results at daily doses of 1,200mg of calcium or more
        alongside 800 IU of vitamin D or more. For a decade that was the number everyone quoted.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then in 2015 Bolland and colleagues went back through it in <em>The BMJ</em> and separated the
        trials by quality. Across 20 trials and 58,573 people, calcium supplements did reduce total
        fracture, risk ratio 0.89. But restricted to the four trials at lowest risk of bias, which
        between them held 44,505 people — the great majority of the evidence — there was no effect on
        fracture at any site. The funnel plot was asymmetric in a direction that favoured calcium,
        which is the statistical signature of small positive studies being published while small
        negative ones are not.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same team's companion paper found that increasing calcium intake raised bone mineral
        density by about 1% — real, measurable, and too small to expect a fracture difference from.
        That is a useful calibration for anyone reading a density report: a number can move without
        the thing you care about moving. We cover how to read those reports in
        <a href="/pages/osteopenia-vs-osteoporosis">osteopenia vs osteoporosis</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dietary calcium told a flatter story still. In the same 2015 review, 14 of 22 observational
        studies found no association between dietary calcium and total fracture, and 17 of 21 found
        none for hip fracture.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is worth saying that not everyone accepts the 2026 verdict. Osteoporosis Canada's 2026
        position statement argues that the authors' threshold for clinical meaningfulness was
        arbitrary, and that a reduction in fractures including hip fractures could still matter at a
        population level. That is a fair objection, and it is why this article says the benefit is
        small rather than absent.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who-benefits">Who does benefit from taking calcium?</h2>

      <p>
        The people the trials recruited least: frail, elderly, housebound or institutionalised
        adults who were short of both calcium and vitamin D to start with.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference trial is Chapuy and colleagues in the <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>
        in 1992, known as Decalyos. It gave 1,200mg of calcium with 800 IU of vitamin D3 to
        institutionalised elderly French women and reduced hip and other non-vertebral fractures;
        the relative risk of hip fracture in the placebo group compared with the treated group was
        about 1.7. A follow-up trial, Decalyos II, reproduced a similar figure. These were women in
        nursing homes with poor sun exposure and low intakes — a different population from a
        community-dwelling 55-year-old in Pune.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction is the whole of it, and it is why the guidance has gone where it has. The US
        Preventive Services Task Force recommends against daily supplementation with 400 IU or less
        of vitamin D and 1,000mg or less of calcium for the primary prevention of fractures in
        community-dwelling postmenopausal women, citing no effect on fracture incidence and an
        increase in kidney stones. For men and premenopausal women it concluded the evidence was
        insufficient to judge. Crucially, none of that guidance applies to people with diagnosed
        osteoporosis, diagnosed deficiency, or a medical reason to supplement.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the group that should probably be taking calcium is narrower than the shelf suggests, but
        it is not empty. It includes people whose diet genuinely does not reach the target, people on
        osteoporosis medication for whom calcium and vitamin D are adjuncts, pregnant and
        breastfeeding women, and people with malabsorption, hypoparathyroidism or osteomalacia.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">How much calcium do Indians actually get?</h2>

      <p>
        Far less than the trial populations did, which changes the arithmetic rather than the
        evidence. Published estimates of average Indian adult intake range from roughly 300 to 460mg
        a day. One review of Indian orthopaedic practice reports an average of about 429mg; other
        sources put the median nearer 300 to 350mg. The ICMR-NIN 2020 recommendation for Indian
        adults is 1,000mg.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 2026 the Endocrine Society of India's Skeletal Health Taskforce published the country's
        first calcium consensus in the <em>Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism</em>,
        written by 22 endocrinologists. Its position is food first, supplements only when needed, and
        intake assessed before anything is prescribed. It notes that dietary calcium is inadequate in
        more than half the Indian population. It advises against elemental calcium above 1,000mg a
        day, and against formulations containing active vitamin D, in the absence of a specific
        indication. For people who avoid dairy it lists ragi, sesame, horse gram, rajma, drumstick
        leaves and curry leaves.
      </p>

      <p>
        The practical arithmetic is simple and most people have never done it. A glass of milk
        carries roughly 250mg. Reaching 1,000mg from dairy alone means about four servings a day. A
        rice-and-dal plate with no curd, no paneer and no ragi will not get close. Vitamin D is the
        larger Indian problem underneath all of this, because calcium absorption depends on it, and
        we have written separately on
        <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency symptoms</a> and on
        <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">what actually increases bone density</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="forms">Calcium carbonate or calcium citrate?</h2>

      <p>
        Citrate is absorbed somewhat better and tolerated better; carbonate is cheaper and more
        concentrated. Both are acceptable, and the Endocrine Society of India consensus recommends
        these two specifically while advising against other calcium salts for lack of evidence and
        higher cost.
      </p>

