      <p>
        One more mechanical point. Absorption efficiency is highest when a single dose is 500mg or
        less, and falls as the load goes up. If you and your doctor have settled on more than that,
        splitting it across the day is the standard advice — though at least one loading study found
        absorption was capacity-limited even when doses were divided, so do not expect splitting to
        rescue a dose that was never needed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="risks">What are the risks of calcium supplements?</h2>

      <p>
        Two are established, one is contested, and all three matter more once you accept that the
        benefit in healthy adults is small.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Kidney stones.</strong> In the Women's Health Initiative trial of calcium plus
          vitamin D in 36,282 postmenopausal women, stones occurred in 2.3% of participants and
          supplementation raised the risk by about 17%. Observational work points the same way, and
          with a twist worth knowing: higher calcium <em>from food</em> is associated with fewer
          stones, while calcium from supplements is associated with more.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gut symptoms.</strong> Constipation and bloating are the common reason people stop,
          and they are dose-dependent and more associated with carbonate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cardiovascular risk — contested.</strong> A reanalysis of the WHI data by Bolland
          and colleagues reported a 13 to 22% increase in cardiovascular events among the women not
          already taking their own calcium, and later meta-analyses from the same group reported
          roughly 20% higher rates of coronary heart disease and heart attack with calcium alone. The
          original trial report did not show an overall cardiovascular increase, these were secondary
          outcomes, and the finding has been disputed since. Calcium from food has not shown the same
          association. We would not call this settled in either direction, but it is a reason not to
          take more than you need.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="skelecoll">How much calcium is in Skelecoll?</h2>

      <p>
        250mg of calcium, with 600 IU of vitamin D3 and 50mg of magnesium, per 30ml shot. That is a
        quarter of the 1,000mg Indian adults are asked to reach, and roughly a fifth of the 1,200mg
        used in the trials that actually reduced hip fractures. It is a co-factor dose, not a bone
        therapy, and this article would be dishonest if it implied otherwise.
      </p>

      <p>
        That framing is deliberate rather than apologetic. Skelecoll's main ingredient is 10,000mg of
        hydrolysed marine collagen; the calcium, D3 and magnesium are there to support the bone side
        of a formula built around collagen, glucosamine and hyaluronic acid. If your dietary calcium
        is around 400mg a day, 250mg is a meaningful contribution towards 1,000mg. If you have
        osteoporosis and a fracture history, you need a doctor, a DEXA scan and in all likelihood a
        prescription medicine — and calcium and vitamin D alongside it as adjuncts, at doses your
        doctor sets, not ours. The same honesty applies to the rest of the label: we have written
        about why Skelecoll contains no <a href="/pages/vitamin-k2-for-bones">vitamin K2</a>.
      </p>

