      <h2 id="doctor">When to see a doctor</h2>

      <p>Book an appointment rather than buying a calcium tablet if you have:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A fracture from a minor fall or knock, particularly at the wrist, hip or spine.</li>
        <li>Height loss, a new stoop, or sudden back pain, which can indicate a spinal fracture.</li>
        <li>A history of kidney stones, kidney disease, or a raised blood calcium result.</li>
        <li>Parathyroid or thyroid disease, sarcoidosis, or long-term steroid use.</li>
        <li>A prescription for levothyroxine, iron, or certain antibiotics — calcium interferes with their absorption, so timing matters.</li>
        <li>A DEXA report you have not had explained, or a T-score at or below -2.5.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="jr-disclaimer">
        This article is for general information and is not medical advice. Nutritional supplements
        are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease. If you are pregnant,
        breastfeeding, taking prescription medication, or have a known fish or shellfish allergy,
        speak to a qualified healthcare professional before starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Are calcium supplements a waste of money?</summary>
          <p>Not a waste, but oversold for healthy adults. The 2026 BMJ review of 69 trials and 153,902 adults found no meaningful fracture or fall benefit from routine supplementation. If your diet already reaches about 1,000mg a day, a tablet adds little. If it reaches 400mg, the tablet is filling a real gap.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much calcium should I take per day?</summary>
          <p>The ICMR-NIN 2020 target for Indian adults is 1,000mg a day from food and supplements combined, not 1,000mg of supplement on top of your diet. The Endocrine Society of India advises against elemental calcium above 1,000mg a day without a specific indication. Estimate what you already eat first.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take calcium with vitamin D?</summary>
          <p>If you are supplementing calcium, yes, because vitamin D governs how much calcium you absorb, and deficiency is widespread in India. In the 2026 review the combination was the only arm to reach statistical significance for fracture, at a risk ratio of 0.91, though the absolute difference was about 1%.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which is better, calcium carbonate or calcium citrate?</summary>
          <p>Citrate is absorbed roughly 22 to 27% better in a pooled analysis and does not need stomach acid, so it suits older adults and anyone on acid-blocking medication. Carbonate is about 40% elemental calcium against citrate's 21%, so it needs fewer tablets and costs less. The Endocrine Society of India recommends either.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do calcium supplements cause kidney stones?</summary>
          <p>They can raise the risk modestly. In the Women's Health Initiative trial of 36,282 postmenopausal women, calcium plus vitamin D increased kidney stones by about 17%, with stones occurring in 2.3% of participants. Calcium from food is associated with fewer stones, not more.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do calcium supplements cause heart problems?</summary>
          <p>This is unresolved. Reanalyses of the Women's Health Initiative by one research group reported 13 to 22% more cardiovascular events, and around 20% higher coronary heart disease with calcium alone, but these were secondary outcomes and the finding is disputed. Dietary calcium shows no such association.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get enough calcium from an Indian vegetarian diet?</summary>
          <p>Yes, but it takes deliberate planning. A glass of milk gives roughly 250mg, so four servings of dairy reaches 1,000mg. Without dairy, the Endocrine Society of India consensus points to ragi, sesame, horse gram, rajma, drumstick leaves and curry leaves. A plain rice-and-dal diet falls well short.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the 250mg of calcium in Skelecoll enough for my bones?</summary>
          <p>On its own, no. 250mg is a quarter of the 1,000mg Indian adults are asked to reach and about a fifth of the 1,200mg used in the trials that reduced hip fractures. It is a co-factor dose that contributes towards a dietary target. Osteoporosis needs a doctor and a prescription, not a supplement.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

